<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adarnase IV of Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/923">923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adarnase IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> was a Bagratid prince of Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. In 888 he took the title king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, restoring Georgian kingship after about three centuries, and ruled until 923. He balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Bagratid kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and his descendants went on to unite the Georgian lands into the Kingdom of Georgia around 1008.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adarnase was the only known son of the Kouropalates David I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, who was murdered in 881 in a conflict with Nasra of Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a>, a young cadet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a>. Once Liparit Baghvashi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-i-of-armenia">Ashot I of Armenia</a> had driven Nasra out, Adarnase succeeded as duke of Lower Tao, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> gave the title of Kouropalates to his cousin Gurgen. He began his rule by building the cathedral of Bana with the architect Kvirike, whom he made its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. His name comes from the Middle Persian Ādurnarsēh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 888 Nasra returned with Abkhazian troops as part of a Byzantine push into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>. Adarnase, backed by Ashot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, Gurgen and Liparit, defeated him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Mtkvari</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> and had him executed at Aspindza. In 891 he and Bagrat I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> beat Gurgen at the Battle of Mglinavi, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> recognised Adarnase as Kouropalates as well as king. That year he crowned Smbat I as king of Armenia at Yerazgavors, and the two kings fought side by side against Arab invaders in 895 and after.</p><p>The alliance broke over Kartli. When Abkhazia invaded it in 904, Adarnase had King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iii-of-abkhazia">Constantine III of Abkhazia</a> seized during talks, but Smbat, fearing a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, released him and let him annex Kartli. In 905 Adarnase plotted to kill Smbat, took Ani and Yerazgavors and failed. He then sided with the Sajid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-abil-saj">Yusuf ibn Abi'l-Saj</a>, whose invasions ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and in 914 his troops captured the fleeing Smbat and handed him to the emir, who had him killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Turning again, Adarnase crowned Smbat's son Ashot II as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 915 with the help of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-abkhazia">George II of Abkhazia</a>, and in his last years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and Armenia aligned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. With his lands reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, he spent the end of his reign building churches. He died in 923 and was succeeded as king by his eldest son David II, while Byzantium gave the title of Kouropalates to his second son Ashot. His line was the direct ancestor of the Bagrationi kings who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> until the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adarnase IV of Iberia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 888 his descendant <b>Adarnase IV</b> took the title king of the Iberians, restoring the royal title in Georgia after three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-iberians">Kingdom of the Iberians</a></li><li><blockquote>In 888 his descendant <b>Adarnase IV</b> revived the royal title, creating the Kingdom of the Iberians.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-iberia">Principality of Iberia</a></li></ul></section>
